Superplastic flow is primarily facilitated by GBS, which involves the sliding of grains over one another, and concurrent accommodation mechanisms that mitigate stress concentrations at triple junctions or grain boundaries, thereby avoiding grain elongation or hardening 2 . These accommodation mechanisms, which include diffusion creep, dislocation activity, and intragranular slip, are vital for preventing stress localization, cavitation, and void nucleation along grain boundaries 20 . Unlike dislocation-mediated plasticity, which occurs within the crystal lattice, GBS is an intergranular process. Since grain boundaries have different energies, sliding is not uniform across all boundaries; thus, triple junctions (where three grains meet) become critical sites for stress concentration and eventual cavitation. However, in M/HEAs, the chemical and structural complexity of boundaries may alter sliding resistance. Thus, superplasticity arises as a cooperative phenomenon governed by the interplay of a stable fine-grained microstructure, efficient diffusion kinetics, and appropriate stress states. When GBS is accommodated efficiently, the material can sustain very high elongations without necking. This behavior is strongly strain-rate dependent and is commonly described by σ = Kε m , where  σ  is the flow stress,  K  is a material constant,  ε ° \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
